# Description
<!-- Please include a summary of the change. -->
<!-- Any details that you think are important to review this PR? -->
<!-- Are there other PRs related to this one? -->

This PR represents a serious refactor of how the pattern system works, to enable more complex behavior (i.e. anchors and capture groups) and codify a more flexible system that can be iterated upon more quickly.

## How it Works

Up until now, almost all rules in Harper have been implemented as `PatternLinters`.
These worked by defining a `Pattern`:

```rust
pub trait Pattern: LSend {
    /// Check if the pattern matches at the start of the given token slice.
    ///
    /// Returns the length of the match if successful, or `None` if not.
    fn matches(&self, tokens: &[Token], source: &[char]) -> Option<usize>;
}
```

The linter would then run this pattern over a given sentence by passing in a sliding window of tokens.
We could string multiple patterns together (using a `SequencePattern`) by simply requiring that multiple patterns must pass, one next to the other.
Simple enough.

There are now two new traits: `Expr` and `Step`:

```rust
/// A common problem in Harper is that we need to identify tokens that fulfil a certain criterion.
/// An `Expr` is a way to express whether a certain set of tokens fulfil that criteria.
/// When supplied a specific position in a token stream, the job of an `Expr` is to determine the window of tokens (including the cursor itself) that fulfils whatever criteria the author desires.
/// It is then the job of another system to identify portions of documents that fulfil this criteria.
pub trait Expr: LSend {
    fn run(&self, cursor: usize, tokens: &[Token], source: &[char]) -> Option<Span>;
}


/// An atomic step within a larger expression.
///
/// Its principle job is to identify (if any) the next position of the cursor.
/// When cursor is moved, all tokens between the current cursor and the target position will be
/// added to the match group.
pub trait Step: LSend {
    fn step(&self, tokens: &[Token], cursor: usize, source: &[char]) -> Option<isize>;
}
```

There are a few observations to be had here.

First: A `Pattern` is simply a special case of a `Step`. A `Pattern` is a `Step` that can only move in the positive direction and does not have access to positional information.
In fact, through a blanket implementation, we can make every `Pattern` fulfill the `Step` trait automatically.
This means we can reuse all of our existing `Pattern`s.

```rust
impl<P> Step for P
where
    P: Pattern,
{
    fn step(&self, tokens: &[Token], cursor: usize, source: &[char]) -> Option<isize> {
        self.matches(&tokens[cursor..], source).map(|i| i as isize)
    }
}
```

Second: notice that the `Step` trait now returns a signed value.
This means we can match text _behind_ the current cursor. Cool, no?

Finally, did you notice that `Expr`s return `Span`s instead of indices?
That means we can create expressions that start in the middle of a pattern and expand outward in either direction.

There are plenty more juicy technical details, but I think they're better represented via code. 
If you're interested, read it.

## What This Means

As I said before, all existing `Pattern`s are also valid `Step`s which are also valid `Expr`s.
From now on, instead of chaining `Pattern`s in a `SequencePattern`, you'll be doing the same thing in a `SequenceExpr`.
Because of the greater flexibility you get with expressions, you'll also have access to the new `AnchorStart` and `AnchorEnd` expressions, which behave similarly to `$` and `^` in RegEx.

